From 1a587c2ae0f1d233c9d451ecf6de3105f2a5a37e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emre Yavuzalp <emreyavuzalp2@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:58:08 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 106/116] econet-eth: address PR review feedback on the PPE/FOE
 offload series

- en75_ppe_find_engine_slot(): drop the IPv6 fallback block that
  recomputed and rescanned the exact same descriptor-scaled candidate
  and full table scan already done unconditionally above it for every
  packet type. It could never find anything the first pass had not
  already ruled out; this only doubled the cost of a miss.
- en75_flow_offload_mangle_eth(): replace #ifdef CONFIG_CPU_BIG_ENDIAN
  / #else with if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_CPU_BIG_ENDIAN)), so both branches
  stay type-checked; add a default case to the inner switch.
- en75_flow_entry::state: enum en75_flow_state instead of a bare int;
  add an explicit EN75_FLOW_INVALID = 0 so a zeroed-but-never-set
  entry reads as invalid instead of aliasing PENDING.
- en75_ppe_offload_replace(): reorder locals (reverse christmas tree).
- Drop stale "milestone 2b-*" references and development-time notes
  that describe earlier, superseded staging behaviour (force-to-CPU)
  which this series no longer implements, or that only make sense as
  commit-log history, not code comments.
- en75_foe_entry_set_ipv6_tuple(): move the address-copy loop into the
  switch cases it belongs to instead of running after it unconditionally.
- econet_ppe.c: drop en75_ppe_dump_forward_regs(), a bring-up-only
  register dump that has no debugfs gating and is no longer needed now
  that PPE bring-up is verified on hardware.
- econet_foe.h: use the static_assert() wrapper instead of a raw
  _Static_assert() (checkpatch); trim comments that explained how a
  fact was confirmed rather than just stating it, and the "not yet
  wired into the datapath" line that no longer reflects this series.
- econet_ppe.h: note what the vendor PARSE_LAYER_INFO magic value's
  bytes mean (IPv4/IPv6/UDP/TCP enables).
- econet_foe.c: note that the 0xa5a5a5 pad pattern is an arbitrary
  sentinel (like 0xdeadbeef), not a meaningful hardware value.
- Clarify in comments why the engine-slot-recovery math assumes
  real_slot is always even (EN75_PPE_HASH_OFFSET: two FOE entries
  share a hash bucket, and only the even bucket-base address is ever
  computed by a hash function; the odd "second way" of a collision is
  the engine's own choice) and that the full scan remains the fallback
  if that assumption is ever violated.

The byte-order handling questioned in review (CONFIG_CPU_BIG_ENDIAN
splits in econet_foe.h/econet_ppe_offload.c, and l2->etype for PPPoE)
is intentional and hardware-verified on both EN751221 (big-endian,
TP-Link Archer C5v) and EN7528 (little-endian, TP-Link EX20v): this
series is not LE-theoretical, EX20v ships and is tested with PPPoE +
hardware offload active together. Likewise "no WED" in the top-of-file
comment refers to EN751221 specifically, not the whole SoC family.

Signed-off-by: Emre Yavuzalp <emreyavuzalp2@gmail.com>
